<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  User-defined custom tag cleanup rules for TagFixer.

  These run as an ADDITIVE second pass, AFTER TagFixer's built-in hardcoded fixes
  (parenthetical stripping, album suffix stripping, artist extraction/casing, genre
  normalization). The built-in fixes are not configurable here and are unaffected by
  anything in this file. Every change a rule makes is labelled "[custom rule: <id>]"
  in the run output so it is distinguishable from a built-in fix.

  A missing or malformed file is not an error: custom rules are simply skipped.
  One invalid rule is skipped on its own; the remaining rules still apply.

  Rule attributes:
    id           Required. Short unique name; appears in the change label.
    field        Required. Which tag to act on: title | album | artists | genre
    match        Required. How to test the field: contains | equals | regex | startsWith | endsWith
    value        The operand for the match test (the substring, exact value, or regex).
    action       Required. What to do when the rule matches: regex-replace | set-value
    pattern      regex-replace only. The regex to replace. Omit to reuse value (handy with match="regex").
    replacement  regex-replace: the replacement text (omit for deletion).
                 set-value: the new value for the field.
    enabled      Optional, default true. Set false to keep a rule on file without running it.

  Matching and replacing are case-insensitive. Rules run in file order, each seeing the
  result of the ones before it. The "artists" field is the semicolon-separated joined form.

  Example (commented out - illustrative only, remove the comment markers to activate):

  <Rule id="strip-bonus-track-suffix"
        field="title"
        match="endsWith"
        value=" [Bonus Track]"
        action="regex-replace"
        pattern="\s*\[Bonus Track\]$"
        replacement="" />
-->
<TagFixCustomRules>

